! mcp_bootstrap_p165.strap -- the REWIND pseudo-op (Z REW ST), its
! shared common-return (Z COM RT), the rewind branch-table slot
! (Z TB RW) and the UNLOAD pseudo-op (Z UNLDB), printed page 165.
! docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.172, header READ and not assumed:
! the top line prints "TIME CLOCK 00054041   PUNID 11D11MCP
! CARD ID IK000001   DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE NUMBER  165".
!
! >>> THE SURVEY MISREAD THIS PAGE'S OWN NUMBER. <<<  The survey
! reported printed p.165 at PDF offset 170 (i.e. it read the header
! as "2"), which would be PDF p.167.  PDF p.172's header reads
! "PAGE NUMBER  165" at 400 dpi with no ambiguity, so the volume's
! +7 offset holds here exactly as everywhere else.  PDF p.167 is
! printed p.160, which is already transcribed.
!
! THE HEADING ROW'S OWN "LOCATION" FIELD IS BLANK, and that is the
! rule and not an omission: line 1 is an asterisk banner card that
! compiles nothing, so there is no line-1 location to print.  The
! field was rendered at 400 dpi across its whole width and carries
! no digits; nothing was read off the dashed rule.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE IS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THIRTY-EIGHT PRINTED LINES, twenty-nine of them located, and the
! paper runs out at line 38 with the form two thirds empty.  Two
! routines separated by a banner:
!
!   lines  1-3   "***** REWIND *****" banner (three comment cards).
!   line   4     SLC,$ -- the counter arriving, 220226.00.
!   lines  5-29  Z REW ST, the REWIND pseudo-operation.
!   lines 30-31  two bare comment cards (the "-" marker only).
!   lines 32-34  "***** UNLOAD *****" banner.
!   line  35     SLC,$ -- the counter arriving, 220244.00.
!   lines 36-38  Z UNLDB, the UNLOAD pseudo-operation.
!
! REWIND, read as code:  Z REW ST saves its caller's IC into the
! branch-table slot Z TB RW+.32 (line 5), which is line 29's own
! half word -- line 29 is printed "B,$" and the SIC overwrites its
! address, which is what the comment "-BRANCH BACK TO MOTHER
! ROUTINE" means.  It then sets the REWIND bit S REW($11) (line 7),
! stores its own continuation into S IO INS($12) (lines 9-10), and
! issues the control order (line 11).  Lines 12-13 are the
! I/O-reject exit, 14-16 the end-of-operation linkage.  Line 17 is
! Z COM RT, the common return shared by REWIND and UNLOAD: it
! checks that the equipment is a tape (lines 17-20), sets the
! evaluation mask and table addresses from Z EVL RW and Z TB RW
! (lines 21-25), clears the unit's UNR status bit and sets its CTL
! bit (lines 23 and 26), and leaves to the status evaluator
! Z STEVL (line 27).
!
! UNLOAD is three cards: the same SIC into the same slot, the same
! branch to Z COM RT, and B,K UNLOD.  Z UNLDB and Z REW ST differ
! only in that third card, which is why the two share Z TB RW.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! COUNTING THE ROWS -- WHERE THIS DISAGREES WITH THE SURVEY
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The survey read 24 rows, 220226.00..220245.00, kind=code.  The
! endpoints are right and the count is not: there are
!
!   29 LOCATED rows   (lines 4-7, 9-29, 35-38)
!   27 rows WITH BINARY OUTPUT   (the two SLC,$ rows emit nothing)
!   23 half words and 4 full words   (lines 7, 11, 23, 26)
!
! Nothing fell off either end.  The count was taken off the CARD
! SERIAL column, not off the row images:  lines 1-31 carry
! IK000001..IK000027 and lines 32-38 carry IL000001..IL000006, so
! the page is complete from its first card to its last.  Line 38 is
! IL000006 and the paper below it is blank.
!
! FIVE CARDS CARRY TWO STATEMENTS EACH, which is why 38 printed
! lines come from 33 cards (27 IK + 6 IL).  The duplicated serials are the
! evidence and they are not an OCR artefact -- rendered at 600 dpi:
!
!   lines  5,6   IK000005   SIC,Z TB RW+.32  ;  B,Z COM RT
!   lines  9,10  IK000008   LX,0,$+1.0       ;  SX,0,S IO INS($12)
!   lines 12,13  IK000010   LVI,1,$+1.0      ;  B,M IO REJ
!   lines 14,15  IK000011   SIC,K EOP R      ;  B,K SEOP T
!   lines 36,37  IL000005   SIC,Z TB RW+.32  ;  B,Z COM RT
!
! The fifth is in the IL deck: lines 36/37 repeat lines 5/6's card
! exactly, which is why the UNLOAD entry is three lines and two
! cards.  Every one of them is a pair whose
! second member MUST follow the first (SIC/branch, LVI/branch,
! LX/SX), so the pairing is the programmer's, not the printer's.
!
! LINE 8 IS A "CNOP" WITH NO LOCATION AND NO BINARY, and that is
! correct: line 7 is a full word ending at 220227.40, so the
! counter is already word-aligned at 220230.00 and the CNOP pads
! nothing.  It is a real card (IK000007) that compiles zero bits.
!
! THE LOCATION-COLUMN ASTERISK appears on THREE rows: lines 4, 25
! and 35.  Lines 4 and 35 are the two SLC,$ rows; line 25 is an
! ordinary "SV,7,S EVL TB($12)".  Recorded, not explained.  No
! hypothesis is offered: printed p.160 already carries two
! asterisks on ordinary rows (its lines 7 and 26), which kills any
! SLC-shaped rule before this page is consulted.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE JOIN BACKWARD
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Line 4 prints "220226.00 *  SLC,$", so THE PAGE ITSELF states
! that the counter arrives from printed p.164 at 220226.00 -- an
! independent reading of the join, printed by IBM's assembler.
!
! >>> BUT THIS DECK THROWS THAT WITNESS AWAY AND MUST SAY SO. <<<
! A deck has to start somewhere, so line 4 is written here as a
! hard "SLC,(8)220226." instead of the printed "SLC,$".  A hard SLC
! cannot disagree with an arriving counter (workflow "WHAT THE
! ORACLE CANNOT SEE", item 2), so NO CHECK ON THIS PAGE TESTS THE
! JOIN TO p.164.  It has to be compared by hand, and the comparison
! is: the volume OCR of printed p.164 (PDF p.171 -- an INDEX, not a
! witness, and p.164's own transcriber decides it) carries
! "36* 220225.40  221261.10 00  B,M IO REJ" as that page's last
! row, a half-word branch, which leaves the counter at 220226.00.
! If p.164's deck lands anywhere else, this page's line 4 is wrong
! and nothing here will have failed.
!
! LINE 35'S SLC,$ IS WRITTEN AS PRINTED, and that one IS a live
! check, internal to this page: strap_asm accepts a bare "SLC,$"
! (measured), so line 35 does not override the counter and the fact
! that lines 36-38 land at 220244.00/.40 and 220245.00 is this
! deck's own arithmetic agreeing with the printed column.  Had the
! REWIND routine above been one half word longer or shorter, line
! 36's check would fail.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE ONE SUBSTITUTION -- LINE 11, AND IT IS LOUD
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> THE PAGE PRINTS "REW(SEOP),0.0($14)".  THIS DECK WRITES
! >>> RESOLVED 2026-11-02: REW IS NOW IMPLEMENTED and the printed
! form stands.  The note below records the substitution this deck
! carried for one commit, and why it was bit-identical.
! >>> "CTL(SEOP),0.0($14),(8)136."  The printed form was NOT what
! >>> is compiled below.
!
! strap_asm REFUSES the printed form.  Measured, verbatim:
!
!     REW(SEOP),0.0($14)
!       -> AssemblerError: unknown data-description mode 'SEOP'
!
! because REW is not in strap/strap_asm.py's IO_FUNCTION_OPS (which
! holds only SPFL and GONG, deliberately -- see the long comment
! there), so "(SEOP)" falls through to the data-description parser.
! That is an ASSEMBLER LIMITATION, not a reading problem: the
! statement column was rendered at 1400 dpi and reads "Z CTL RW
! REW(SEOP),0.0($14)" with no doubtful glyph.
!
! THE SUBSTITUTE IS EXACT, NOT APPROXIMATE, and it is checked:
!
!     CTL(SEOP),0.0($14),(8)136.  ->  000000.00 8E  000136.15 00
!     the page prints              ->  000000.00 8E  000136.15 00
!
! so the deck's statement and its !CHECK do not contradict each
! other, which is the condition rule 3 sets.  TWO independent
! sources make the equivalence an identity rather than a guess:
!
!   (a) 0o136 = Rewind.  The Reference Manual's IBM 729 IV control
!       code table (printed p.132), transcribed in
!       docs/exchange_and_io.md, line 626: "136 | Rewind (EOP given
!       at initiation; CS when load point reached)".
!   (b) The page prints the code itself.  The second half word's
!       address field IS 000136, and its .15 is the CTL(SEOP)
!       operation -- byte for byte printed p.152 line 44's
!       "000000.00 8E  000000.15 00" for "CTL(SEOP),0.0($14),0.0",
!       the same instruction with a zero control code.
!
! WHAT IS LOST BY THE SUBSTITUTION: nothing in the bits, and the
! spelling "REW" as an accepted STRAP-II input.  If REW is ever
! added to IO_FUNCTION_OPS (0o136, by (a) and (b) above -- the same
! two-source standard SPFL met), this line should become the
! printed form and this comment should go.
!
! MCP'S OWN IDIOM FOR THE THIRD OPERAND, for whoever does that:
! printed p.164 line 33 writes the code as a BARE DECIMAL bit
! address -- its volume OCR reads "33* 220223.00  000000.00 8E
! 000117.05 00  CTL,0.0($14),79.0", and 79 decimal is 0o117.  So
! "(8)136." here and "94.0" there are the same word; the (8) form
! was chosen because docs/transcription_workflow.md blesses it and
! because it prints as the page prints.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! AN ENCODING RESULT THIS PAGE MEASURES -- AND WHAT PINS Z EVL RW
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! A VALUE OPERATION ENCODES ITS REGISTER AS 2N+1, ADDED INTO THE
! PRINTED BIT-OFFSET FIELD.  Eight rows on this page, three
! registers, four operations, nothing else varying:
!
!     line 12  LVI,1,$+1.0            220233.03    0o03 = 2*1+1
!     line 17  V-I,2,.32              000000.45    0o40 + 0o05
!     line 18  SV,2,S TIC             215265.05    0o05 = 2*2+1
!     line 19  KVI,4,4.0              000004.11    0o11 = 2*4+1
!     line 21  LVI,7,Z EVL RW         221153.57    0o40 + 0o17
!     line 22  SV,7,S EVL MK($12)     000002.57    0o40 + 0o17
!     line 24  LVI,7,Z TB RW          220243.17    0o17 = 2*7+1
!     line 25  SV,7,S EVL TB($12)     000002.17    0o17 = 2*7+1
!
! and it agrees with printed p.160 line 11's LVI,9 printing 0o63
! (0o40 + 0o23, 0o23 = 2*9+1) on a .40 symbol.  The 0o40 is the
! operand's own bit 32.
!
! >>> THIS IS WHAT PINS Z EVL RW AT 221153.40 AND NOT .00. <<<
! Lines 21 and 24 are the SAME OPERATION ON THE SAME REGISTER and
! they print 0o57 and 0o17.  Z TB RW is this page's own label at
! 220243.00, so 0o17 is the no-bit-32 reading and 0o57 carries
! bit 32: Z EVL RW is a .40 symbol.  That is an OBSERVATION off two
! rows of one page, not the usual ".0 by convention".  It is
! corroborated by an INDEX (not a witness) -- the volume OCR of the
! untranscribed STATUS EVALUATION page whose first located row is
! 221144.40 = Z STEVL carries
! "221153.40  002000.00+   Z EVL RW   VF,(8)2000.  -REW,UNLD,FREE
! MASK - SEL", a VF constant at exactly 221153.40.
!
! >>> THE RULE DOES NOT COVER LX/SX, AND THIS IS UNRESOLVED. <<<
! Lines 9 and 10 are index operations, not value operations:
!
!     line  9  LX,0,$+1.0             220231.00 10    offset 0o00
!     line 10  SX,0,S IO INS($12)     000004.01 1C    offset 0o01
!
! SX,0 fits (0o01 = 2*0+1); LX,0 does not (0o00, where the rule
! predicts 0o01).  The BYTES are not the puzzle -- both are the same
! base byte 10 and line 10's low nibble is just its index $12 (see
! GLYPHS (2) below) -- it is the OFFSET field that disagrees.  One
! page cannot settle whether LX simply does not carry the register
! in that field or whether something else about a word-granular
! fetch zeroes it, and this deck does not guess.  A page carrying LX
! on a register other than 0 would settle it in one row.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! GLYPHS SETTLED, AND HOW
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! (1) LINE 23'S SECOND HALF WORD prints "001000.CO FC" to the eye.
!     It is "001000.00 F0".  The octal field itself prints a C for
!     a 0 ("001000.CO"), which shows the row drops ink on round
!     glyphs; what settles the byte is printed p.160, which carries
!     the SAME CONNECTIVE TWICE -- line 17 "CM0000,S LOC SU" and
!     line 26 "CM0000(BU,1),S SEL($11)", both printing
!     "001000.00 F0" -- against its own lines 31's CM1111
!     "001000.36 F0".  Lines 7 and 26 here print the CM1111 form
!     and both read "001000.36 F0" cleanly, so the page itself
!     carries the contrast.  The assembler agrees.
!
! (2) THE LOW NIBBLE OF A BYTE IS THE INDEX REGISTER, and that is
!     what decides four of this page's bytes -- NOT the glyph.
!     $11 -> B (lines 7, 26), $12 -> C (lines 10, 22, 25), $13 -> D
!     (line 23), $14 -> E (line 11); printed p.152 line 44 and
!     printed p.160 line 13 ("000005.00 8C" for "SIC,S RET AD($12)")
!     read the same nibble the same way.
!
!     >>> THIS DECK'S FIRST READING GOT FOUR OF THEM WRONG IN THE
!     >>> SAME DIRECTION, so the correction is worth recording.  At
!     900 dpi lines 10, 22 and 25 were transcribed "10", "30", "30"
!     -- a reflex application of "a round glyph in a damaged row is
!     a 0".  THE DRUM WAS RIGHT AND THE READER WAS WRONG: at 1400
!     dpi the second glyph of each is visibly OPEN against line 9's
!     and line 18's visibly CLOSED 0, and every one of them has a
!     ($12) that the C spells.  The printed bytes are 1C, 3C, 3C.
!     The asymmetry "dropout opens a closed glyph but never closes
!     an open one" says a C MAY be a damaged 0; it does not say it
!     is, and four rows on one page is the price of treating it as
!     if it did.
!
! (2a) LINE 20'S "C0" IS THE ONE BYTE ON THIS PAGE THAT INK CANNOT
!     SETTLE AT ALL, and it must be said plainly.  Lines 13, 16 and
!     28 are plain branches whose byte IS 00, and at 1400 dpi all
!     three print their first glyph OPEN, exactly as line 20 does;
!     lines 27 and 29 print the same 00 with it CLOSED.  So on this
!     page an open first glyph carries NO information and "C0"
!     cannot be read off the paper.  It is settled by WHAT PRODUCED
!     IT: BZXE's byte is C0, and printed p.159 line 6 is the SAME
!     STATEMENT -- "BZXE,K ILEGL" printing "220064.32 C0", the same
!     six octal digits and the same byte, transcribed by a different
!     reader off a different page.  Fourteen landed decks print BZXE
!     with C0, printed p.124 line 55 read at 1800 dpi, and printed
!     p.153 line 45 prints BXE as "217667.32 C2" against it, so the
!     C is a real hex C and the low bits are the condition, not ink.
!
! (3) "S CCW", not "S COW" or "S CCH".  Rendered at 1400 dpi the
!     name reads "S CCW.18($13)"; the drum's W is the glyph that
!     looks lowercase (the same one in "REWIND" in the banner and
!     in "Z TB RW").  The comment column is the oracle: "-TURN OFF
!     UNR STATUS BIT IN CW".  And eleven landed decks carry SCCW at
!     4.0 while nothing in the repository carries SCOW.
!
! (4) "Z UNLDB", read at 1400 dpi: the D has the straight left stem
!     and squared corners that separate it from a C, and the final
!     B has both bowls and the stroke.  Nothing references this
!     label yet, so it rests on ink alone -- flagged accordingly.
!
! (5) ".18" IN LINE 23 IS DECIMAL, as a bare STRAP bit offset
!     always is, and the page confirms it: S CCW is 4.0 and the
!     printed address field is "000004.22", 0o22 = 18.
!
! (6) "V-I" ON LINE 17, at 1400 dpi, with a clean closed O in the
!     name "Z COM RT" beside it.  strap_asm's own comment (line
!     3500) records "V-I,$X15,1.0 -> 1.37 0D"; this row prints
!     byte 0D, which is the same operation.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! EXTERNALS -- EVERY ONE CARRIED AS A LANDED DECK WRITES IT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! A connective or VFL operation writing no data description takes
! its length from the SYMBOL, so a dds that disagrees with the
! defining page refuses the merge.  LINE 26 IS EXACTLY THAT CASE
! ("CM1111,S CTL($11)", no dds), so S CTL's "SYN(BU,1), 0.60" is
! printed p.99 line 48's own card, copied field for field and not
! normalised.  The same for the other five dds-bearing externals.
!
! The three "SYN, (8)nnnn" externals reached only through a BRANCH
! (M IO REJ, K SUPP, K SEOP T, K ILEGL, Z STEVL, K K LOC, K UNLOD)
! are known TO THE HALF WORD ONLY -- a branch shows bit 32 and no
! lower bit.  Writing ".0" on them is a convention; bit 32 is read.
! K EOP R is the exception: line 14 is a SIC, which prints the full
! 24-bit address, so 221354.40 is observed to all six digits.

SREW    SYN(BU,1), 0.52              ! p.99  line 43 -REWIND TAPE
SCTL    SYN(BU,1), 0.60              ! p.99  line 48 -CONTROL - OPERATION
SIOINS  SYN(BU,64), 4.0              ! p.101 line 17 -IO INSTR. LAST
SEVLTB  SYN(BU,19), 2.0              ! p.101 line 11 -EVALUATION TABLE BASE
SEVLMK  SYN(BU,19), 2.32             ! p.101 line 12 -EVALUATION MASK
SCCW    SYN(BU,64), 4.0              ! p.102 line 15 -COPIED CONTROL WORD
STIC    SYN, (8)215265.0             ! STored Instruction Counter
KSUPP   SYN, (8)216242.0             ! half word only
KILEGL  SYN, (8)220064.0             ! printed p.160 line 8's own label
KKLOC   SYN, (8)220065.40            ! printed p.160 line 11's own label
MIOREJ  SYN, (8)221261.0             ! half word only
ZSTEVL  SYN, (8)221144.40            ! half word only; eight pages agree
ZEVLRW  SYN, (8)221153.40            ! the .40 is MEASURED -- see above
KSEOPT  SYN, (8)221350.40            ! half word only
KEOPR   SYN, (8)221354.40            ! a SIC: all 24 bits observed
KUNLOD  SYN, (8)221733.0             ! half word only; line 38 is a branch

        SLC, (8)220226.              ! line  4 printed "220226.00 *  SLC,$"
                                     !         -- A HARD ORIGIN SUBSTITUTED
                                     !         FOR A PRINTED SLC,$; the join
                                     !         to p.164 is UNCHECKED here

ZREWST  SIC,ZTBRW+.32                ! line  5
        B,ZCOMRT                     ! line  6  same card as 5
        CM1111(BU,1),SREW($11)       ! line  7
        CNOP                         ! line  8  compiles nothing
        LX,0,$+1.0                   ! line  9
        SX,0,SIOINS($12)             ! line 10  same card as 9
ZCTLRW  REW(SEOP),0.0($14)           ! line 11  printed form -- REW landed 2026-11-02
                                     !          -- SUBSTITUTED, see above
        LVI,1,$+1.0                  ! line 12
        B,MIOREJ                     ! line 13  same card as 12
        SIC,KEOPR                    ! line 14
        B,KSEOPT                     ! line 15  same card as 14
        B,KSUPP                      ! line 16
ZCOMRT  V-I,2,.32                    ! line 17
        SV,2,STIC                    ! line 18
        KVI,4,4.0                    ! line 19
        BZXE,KILEGL                  ! line 20
        LVI,7,ZEVLRW                 ! line 21
        SV,7,SEVLMK($12)             ! line 22
        CM0000(BU,1),SCCW.18($13)    ! line 23
        LVI,7,ZTBRW                  ! line 24
        SV,7,SEVLTB($12)             ! line 25  (the third asterisk)
        CM1111,SCTL($11)             ! line 26
        B,ZSTEVL                     ! line 27
ZTBRW   B,KKLOC                      ! line 28
        B,$                          ! line 29  the slot line 5 overwrites

        SLC,$                        ! line 35 printed "220244.00 *  SLC,$"
                                     !         -- WRITTEN AS PRINTED

ZUNLDB  SIC,ZTBRW+.32                ! line 36
        B,ZCOMRT                     ! line 37  same card as 36
        B,KUNLOD                     ! line 38

! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE PRINTED LISTING, recorded so the oracle can check it later
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
!   line  1                                                                   - ****************************************  IK000001
!   line  2                                                                   -*************** R E W I N D ************  IK000002
